<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pennine Alps</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Valais Alps</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pennine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> are a range of the western Alps along the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-st-bernard-pass">Great St Bernard Pass</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simplon-pass">Simplon Pass</a>. They include many of the highest peaks of the Alps, among them Monte Rosa and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matterhorn">Matterhorn</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> they formed the south-eastern limit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burgundy">Kingdom of Upper Burgundy</a>, whose passes linked it with Italy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pennine-alps.jpg" alt="Pennine Alps" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Grindel1 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Valais_Alps.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
